<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glasgow</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Glasgow is the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-clyde">River Clyde</a>. Tradition makes it the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kentigern">Saint Kentigern</a>'s church in the 6th century, and it grew around its medieval cathedral and the university founded in 1451. From the 18th century it became a great trading port, and in the 19th century one of the leading industrial cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, known for shipbuilding and engineering.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glasgow.jpg" alt="Glasgow" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glasgow_-_aerial_-_2025-04-17_14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Overseas commerce grew rapidly under the Navigation Acts, which reserved colonial trade for British ships: sugar from Jamaica and Barbados, tobacco from Virginia and Maryland and textiles from India were re-exported across Europe, and <b>Glasgow</b> prospered on tobacco after the union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The church was rich, with great reformed abbeys such as Melrose, Kelso and Holyrood, and the founding of universities at St Andrews (1413), <b>Glasgow</b> (1451) and Aberdeen (1495) gave Scotland three universities when England still had two.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Chartism was launched in 1838 with large meetings in Birmingham, <b>Glasgow</b> and the north of England.</blockquote><a href="/movement/chartism">Chartism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scottish Enlightenment flourished in Edinburgh, <b>Glasgow</b> and Aberdeen, the Italian Enlightenment in Milan and Naples, and in Prussia Kant published his Critique of Pure Reason in 1781.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Cotton mills, ironworks and coal mines gathered great numbers of wage-earners in towns such as Manchester and <b>Glasgow</b>, where men, women and children worked days of twelve hours or more under factory discipline, with no protection against injury, sickness or unemployment.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In July 1867 the Syracuse Daily Courier quoted a line of it, said to be sung for hauling the halyards on a steamship bound from New York to <b>Glasgow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/blow-the-man-down">Blow the Man Down</a></li></ul></section>
